       1.3.7 Research capsh binary for potential privilege escalation - [completed] {Findings: `capsh` Binary: It is a tool used to manage and manipulate Linux capabilities. It can be abused or misused in privilege escalation scenarios if an attacker gains control of a system where `capsh` or other binaries have been configured with excessive or insecure capabilities. Linux Capabilities: They break down the vast set of privileges traditionally associated with the root user into smaller, distinct units. This allows for more granular control over what specific actions a process or binary can perform. Privilege Escalation: Misconfigurations or vulnerabilities can lead to privilege escalation, which can occur due to excessive capabilities on binaries, SUID/SGID binaries with capabilities, misconfigured capability bounding sets, and kernel vulnerabilities. `capsh` and Privilege Escalation: `capsh` can be abused if it or another binary it can interact with has elevated capabilities assigned to it. It could potentially be used to spawn a privileged shell or execute commands with elevated privileges. Relevant Capabilities for Privilege Escalation: `CAP_SYS_ADMIN`, `CAP_SETUID`, `CAP_SETGID`, `CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`, `CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH`, `CAP_CHOWN`, and `CAP_FOWNER`. Detection and Prevention: Enumerate capabilities using tools like `getcap` and `capsh --print`, audit for misconfigurations, follow the principle of least privilege, and keep the kernel updated. Further References: GTFOBins, Linux Man Pages, Hacking Articles - Linux Privilege Escalation using Capabilities, Elastic - Unlocking Power Safely: Privilege Escalation via Linux Process Capabilities, Securium Solutions - Linux Privilege Escalation using Capabilities, Juggernaut-Sec - Capabilities – Linux Privilege Escalation.}
